Date: Wed, 10 Mar 93 18:38:30 CST
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: ECON/ALIEN: Invasion of the Self-Owned Organizations

How about this as an example of generating a self-owned corporation.
You set up a trust arrangement with law firm A to hold some funds,
in trust for Self Owned, Inc.  firm A is given some instructions
to follow at a later date.

Step 2: Firm A, following instructions, is directed to set up
a trust arrangement with law firm B as representatives for the
company, using their name as a cover for the company (I'm told
that this type of arrangment is often used to shield owners
from being on the records).

Step 3: Firm B, following enclosed instructions, is directed to
buy all available shares of Self Owned, Inc. from existing owner,
as representatives of firm A.  Existing owner is now out of loop,
and the company is operated in it's own interest by the firm B,
who actually makes day-to-day decisions.  Firm B may receive
instructions from the president of Self-owned Inc (if it has
human employees) by way of Firm A.

Real World Near-Example:

Hughes Aircraft, now owned by General Motors, for about 20 years
was owned by the Howard Hughes Medical Foundation.  The foundation
didn't particularly want to do a lot of medical research, since
it's board consisted of the senior officers of Hughes Aircraft.
The Foundation received a small amount of research money ( a few
million per year).  The company, without any need to produce
current earnings and dividends, simply plowed profits back into
the company.

Hughes was a leader in several areas because it could afford to
take a longer view of investment (not caring about quarterly
earnings or stock price).  

This cosy arrangement changed in the mid-80's when I believe
the government forced the HHMI to sell it's stock and start
acting like a regular foundation, which must spend a certain
portion of it's assets each year on it's stated purpose, and
must diversify from owning only one company's stock.

Dani Eder

