Date: Wed, 10 Mar 1993 21:16:07 -0600
From: extr@jido.b30.ingr.com (Craig Presson)
Subject: REVIEW: _The Nanotech Chronicles_

Michael Flynn, _The Nanotech Chronicles_, Baen Books
(original paperback) 1991, $US 4.95.

This novel reads like a series of shorts and novellas, artificially
strung together by an absentee narrator -- we begin as one Pavel
Denisovitch D'Arrbeu calls up stories from the archives to instruct and
entertain his elementary school pupils aboard an orbital habitat,
and are shown a series of vignettes from the future history of
nanotechnology.

The book is not intended to be a Grand Unified Speculation on its
subject matter, but within the scope the author has chosen, it is
both compelling fiction and decent futurology. While Flynn is at
great pains to explore the action and reaction between the new
technology and the conservative culture, I believe that he finally
does underestimate the long-term impact.

The technical detail in the book is good enough to be
thought-provoking. The best vignette is the first long one, the tale
of Charles Randolph Singer of Singer Labs and the attempt to create
the "rad nanny", a nanite which will increase the resistance of H.
sapiens to radiation. Of course, the US Gummint gets wind of the
project and takes it over -- as a military asset. Singer's eventual
solution is logical within the story, but horrifying from a
Libertarian perspective. I will stop short of any spoilers here.

Some of the other sections are wildly imaginative and even funny.
At 5 FeRNs and 339 pages, I have to give it a thumbs-up.
