Dale Worley writes:
>>>Then along came the railroad, the automobile, the suburbs, and the
nationwide job market.  Suddenly, the number of poor people living
near you became a significant determinant of your well-being --[. . .]
So people started "voting with their feet",[. . .] pressures on communities to
reduce the amount of welfare/mutual-aid <<<

Are we talking about the same thing? 
Here is my understanding of the problem. Up until about 1955, even in very poor
neighborhoods, there was a sort of PPL in effect. Serious juvenile delinquency,
drug abuse, unwed motherhood, etc. were not non-existent, but they were dealt
with informally and effectively. While the cops were never these people's
friends, there was at least a feeling that crooks had something to fear from
the cops but honest people didn't. Beginning in the mid 50's things changed
rapidly for the worse while government spending increased. It wasn't the flight
of the middle class out of these neighborhoods that changed them, since they
were pretty much uniformly working poor to begin with. For some reason, these
people stopped 'doing for themselves.' Why?
I admit my question is fuzzy. What I have is vague memories of anecdotal data
supplied by Walter Williams and Charles Murray. 

I think Dale is talking about some other phenomenon. Dale's hypothesis seems to
be that reduced exit costs eroded community. He points to railroad, autos, etc.
Since the breakdown of community became most noticeable in the 1950's and 60's,
we have to assume that there's some lag between the causes (mid 19th century)
and the effects (mid-late 20th century). 
My hypothesis is that government intervention eroded community. The timing here
is also bad, since the most obvious interventions seem to have begun in the mid
to late 60's, after the process of erosion is already apparent.

Am I the only one who's read Murray? Can anyone help me out? Is this all old
hat?

