> I wonder whether the
> various local acolytes of rabid scientism can possibly dissuade themselves
> from going nonlinear over any subject which is unsusceptible to measurement
> with a micrometer and a three beam scale.

I think you misinterpret our reactions. I don't think we care particularly
about the tools used -- we care about the scientific method. For instance,
I can't measure my capacity to program with a micrometer or a three
beam scale, but I can show anyone that I can write programs on demand.
Yogi's can't fly on demand -- this makes it much harder to believe the
assertion that they can fly. Similarly, assuming that it is really
possible to access past lives with hypnosis it should be trivial to do
an experiment to prove that the technique is real. Select a hundred
subjects at random and keep hypnotizing until you find one who can not
only describe life in 19th century china but who can speak cantonese.
If you properly design the experiment, you should be able to meet all the
needed criteria for reproducability and the like. However, somehow, this
has never been successfully done.

